tests that are yet to be implemented:

Missing Test Coverage (after your exclusions)
1. Worker / infrastructure

Init with an empty database name (should error)

Init with a very long database name (1k+ chars)

Timeout/hang: worker_do_work never resolves – the suite should eventually fail, not freeze forever

Queue error propagation: if one request fails, do later calls still work or is the queue broken?

Worker sending an unexpected first message (not "ready") – does it hang?

2. The drop_table command

Happy path: ["drop_table", "some_table"] – currently never tested with a valid table name (only the missing‑arg error case is covered)

After dropping, verify via list_tables and get_data that the table is gone

3. Data types & constraints

REAL and BLOB columns – insert, read back, swap

UNIQUE constraint on a non‑primary‑key column (e.g., username must be unique)

Composite unique constraints (if supported)

DEFAULT values that are not simple strings (e.g., INTEGER DEFAULT 10)

Explicit NULL insertion – can you send null or the string "NULL"? Does it store actual NULL vs. empty string?

Editing or deleting the primary key column – what happens? (should probably be forbidden)

4. get_data filters & edge cases

Filters beyond id=value: id>2, name='Alice', age>=30, invalid filter syntax, etc.

Empty column list – define expected behaviour (error or empty result) and assert it

Requesting a column that doesn’t exist – should error

get_data on a table with special characters in its name (spaces, quotes)

5. insert_data edge cases

Insert with an explicit id value that collides with an existing auto‑increment counter (after a delete, does it reuse the old id?)

Insert into a table with many columns (e.g., 20+ columns) – stress the serialisation

Insert with column/value count mismatch: already tested, but also try with null/undefined values in the array (do they get coerced to "null"?).

6. swap_columns edge cases

Swapping a column that contains a single quote (already tested) – good.

Swapping across multiple columns in one call (if supported)

Swapping a UNIQUE constrained value that would cause a conflict (should error)

7. Schema introspection (check_table)

Table with special characters in its name (quotes, spaces) – does the SQL break?

After creating an index (even if unimplemented, does the schema still look correct?)

8. Table/column naming

Table name that is a SQL keyword (already tested select as column name) – what about a table named table or index?

Extremely long table/column names (1k characters) – do they work or error cleanly?

9. Concurrency & ordering

Truly parallel worker_do_work calls: fire a batch without await, let them race, then verify final state is consistent

Multiple workers sharing the same sahpool database (if that’s a supported scenario) – otherwise skip

10. Stress / limits

1000+ rows in one table (already did 100, maybe larger)

Many tables (50+) – does list_tables still return all of them correctly?

Very large single value (1 MB text) – may be impractical but shows memory handling

11. Cleanup & termination

Explicit close of the database connection (if the wrapper exposes one) – verify no errors and that later operations correctly fail

Full test‑suite isolation: some tests still depend on the people table from earlier – consider resetting state entirely before each test or at least verifying no leftover tables

Worker termination (myWorker.terminate()) and then trying to use it – should yield clear errors, not hang

12. Stub for future index work

create_index on a valid table/column returns ["error", "not implemented"] (or whatever your placeholder is) – just so it’s documented that the feature is pending.

---

potential issues generated by ai:

1. get_data: empty column list – what happens?
Why it “just passes”: The test logs the payload with console.log but never throws an error, even if the worker returns a bogus success or completely wrong data. It always reaches the end and logs a pass.

Effect: It cannot detect a regression or a bug in the handling of empty column lists.

2. insert_data: empty columns/values – expect default row or error
Why it “just passes”: If the command returns an error, the test returns early and passes immediately. Only when the command succeeds does it check that exactly one row was inserted. The test therefore accepts an error as a valid pass state, even if the intended behavior is to insert a default row.

Effect: If the implementation is changed to reject empty inserts (intentionally or due to a bug), the test will still pass, masking the change.

3. get_data: non-existent table should error (or return empty?)
Why it “just passes”: The test explicitly states that either an error or an empty array is acceptable. It passes if the worker returns ["error", ...], and it also passes if the worker returns ["ok", []]. Only a non‑error with a non‑empty payload would fail.

Effect: It does not enforce a specific contract; the developer can change the error handling without the test ever failing.

4. create_table: table name with spaces and quotes works
Why it “just passes”: If the create_table call itself returns an error, the test immediately returns and passes, never checking list_tables or verifying the table’s existence. It only validates the special‑character support when creation succeeds.

Effect: If the system should support such names but a regression breaks it, the test will silently pass.

5. create_table: column name is a SQL keyword (select)
Why it “just passes”: Identical pattern: if create_table fails, the test returns early and logs a pass. The schema check is only performed on success.

Effect: A regression that breaks keyword‑quoted column names would go unnoticed.

6. composite UNIQUE constraint (if supported) prevents duplicate pairs
Why it “just passes”: The test tries to insert a duplicate pair and then does nothing with the reply. Whether the duplicate insert succeeds or errors, the test always ends without throwing. The comment inside the test even says “we’ll just log that it passed without error; the test itself doesn’t fail”.

Effect: This is a pure documentation test – it can never fail, no matter how the system behaves.

7. get_data: table with spaces and quotes in name
Why it “just passes”: If creating the table fails, the test returns early and passes. Only when the table is created successfully does it test get_data.

Effect: Once again, if the feature is missing or broken, the test still reports a pass.

8. check_table: table with spaces and quotes in name
Why it “just passes”: Same pattern – if create_table errors, the test returns and passes without ever running check_table.

Effect: You don’t learn whether check_table works with special‑character table names unless the creation already succeeds.

9. table named 'table' (SQL keyword)
Why it “just passes”: On a create_table error the test returns and passes, skipping all further checks.

Effect: A regression that breaks the quoting of reserved words would not be caught.

10. table named 'index' (SQL keyword)
Why it “just passes”: Identical conditional‑return‑and‑pass logic.

Effect: Same masking of failures.

11. extremely long table name (1k chars)
Why it “just passes”: If create_table fails (e.g., OS limit, identifier too long), the test returns and passes. The later insertion and read‑back tests are never reached.

Effect: It does not guarantee that long names are handled; an unexpected rejection is still considered a pass.

12. extremely long column name (1k chars)
Why it “just passes”: Same pattern – if create_table fails, the test passes.

Effect: Same masking issue.

What makes a test “pass just to pass” vs. a proper test?
A proper test defines one expected outcome (or a small set of well‑defined outcomes) and fails when the implementation deviates.
A test that returns early on error, accepts multiple contradicting outcomes, or never throws is not guarding correctness; it’s merely documenting that the system didn’t crash. These tests should be rewritten to enforce the desired contract, or at least be marked as “exploratory / not‑verifying” so they don’t give a false sense of security.
